                <pb facs="0034" n="26" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg26"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.4131/1405: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-04-11T16:00:00+01:00">April 11, 1938—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 11—11:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>295. Department’s 143, April 9, 1 p.m. A member of my staff was the guest at
                    luncheon today of the senior Board of Trade official who handles the American
                    trade agreement requests. The latter brought up the question of a concession on
                    Douglas fir and enumerated the difficulties inherent in the situation. In reply
                    the Department’s position that concession on Douglas fir was indispensable was
                    reiterated and emphasized.</p>
                <p>The impression was gained that the Board of Trade was sounding us out. It is
                    awaiting a further report from Overton before “making up its mind” but the
                    effect upon the Scandinavian countries of a concession to us is being
                    weighed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennedy</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
